numericWeightEDDegree -- numerically compute ED degrees of affine cones using homotopy continuation
***************************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        UED = numericUnitEDDegree(F,G)
        GED = numericGenericEDDegree(F,G)
        GED = homotopyEDDegree(F,G,U,V)
  * Inputs:
      * F, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a system of polynomials (need not
        be square) defining the variety
      * G, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a system of polynomials (complete
        intersection) such that $V(F)$ is an irreducible component of $V(G)$,
        i.e. a witness model
      * U, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a (generic) data vector
      * W, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a (generic) weight vector
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Submodel => a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, default value {}, list
        of linear polynomials to slice the variety
      * *note TempDirectory: TempDirectory, => ..., default value null, set
        directory for Bertini runs
  * Outputs:
      * ED, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, a generic/unit Euclidean
        distance degree

Description
===========

Special instances of the *note homotopyEDDegree: homotopyEDDegree, method that
uses a homotopy on weights. The unit variant of this method computes an ED
degree using random (complex) data and unit weights, whereas
`numericGenericEDDegree` will use random data and random weights.

+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y];                                               |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : F = G = {x^2+y^2-1};                                       |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : (U,W) = ({.12, .23}, {.15, .331});                         |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : UED = numericUnitEDDegree(F, G)                            |
|                                                                |
|o4 = 2                                                          |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : GED = numericGenericEDDegree(F, G)                         |
|-- warning: experimental computation over inexact field begun   |
|--          results not reliable (one warning given per session)|
|                                                                |
|o5 = 4                                                          |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : GED = numericWeightEDDegree(F, G, U, W)                    |
|                                                                |
|o6 = 4                                                          |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

Inaccurate results may be returned if $V(F)$ is contained in $V(L)$. The
computed ED degree may be lower than expected due to path tracking.

